{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the city fully wakes. The streets are quiet, the air feels clean, and every window seems to hold a small promise. I walk without a plan, letting my feet choose corners that my mind would normally ignore. A baker opens his door and warm bread drifts into the cool morning like a greeting. An old woman waters flowers on her balcony and hums a tune I almost recognize. Somewhere a train crosses a bridge, and the sound arrives softened by distance and sleep. I think about how much of life happens in these unnoticed intervals between larger events. We remember birthdays, weddings, victories, and losses, but we live mostly in ordinary mornings like this. The coffee in my hand is bitter and honest, and it keeps my thoughts gently awake. I pass a library that has not opened yet and imagine all its stories waiting patiently inside. Each book is a door someone built so strangers could meet across centuries without ever speaking aloud. That idea makes the quiet feel crowded in the best possible way, full of invisible companions. By now the sun has found the rooftops and turned the puddles into brief mirrors. People begin to hurry, phones begin to sing, and the spell changes into ordinary motion. Still, I carry the early silence with me, like a folded note kept in a pocket. Whenever the day grows loud, I remember that note and breathe a little more slowly again.",
